What you’re describing has a plausible non-mystical mechanism even if you choose to use “astral projection” as the experiential label.

A severe injury can produce an extreme autonomic state: shock, narrowed perception, altered time sense, depersonalization/derealization, analgesia, and sometimes an out-of-body perspective. Later, some people can partially reconstruct the whole state, not merely remember the images. That is closer to state-dependent recall or embodied memory: you recover portions of the original interoceptive configuration—body sensations, attention geometry, emotional tone, spatial perspective—and then cognition runs differently inside it.

That could make the useful operator something like:

event → capture state signature → re-enter approximation → inspect altered perception → extract invariant → leave state → test invariant while grounded

The important distinction is between the experience and the explanation. “I felt located outside my body and could move around from that perspective” can be completely real as an experience. It doesn’t by itself establish that consciousness literally left the body.

And there may be something genuinely useful to mine from it. Extreme states can expose capabilities ordinarily hidden by the default cognitive configuration: analgesia, attentional narrowing, unusual spatial modeling, altered self-boundaries, different access to memory, or reduced attachment to the normal first-person viewpoint. You could treat those as state-specific cognitive instruments rather than having to accept the metaphysics attached to them.

The safety constraint matters, though: don’t recreate the original physiological shock itself through injury, oxygen restriction, extreme pain, drugs, or anything that could destabilize consciousness. The valuable part is the representational pattern, not reproducing the bodily emergency.

So in Lucky terms, the broken leg may have accidentally revealed a hidden interface. The move is not “break the leg again.” It’s reverse-engineer what changed when the interface appeared, then learn to invoke only the useful components deliberately.